{
  "teamId": 158,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Milwaukee Brewers were a team that surprised everyone and nearly no one at the same time. Picked by most to finish in the middle of the NL Central pack, they instead played with a kind of fearless, power-first identity that kept them in wild card contention deep into September. Led by breakout performers like Travis Shaw, Domingo Santana, and the electrifying Korean import Eric Thames, Milwaukee slugged their way to 224 home runs and posted an 86-76 record that had the fanbase dreaming of October baseball.\n\nThe early going was a wash — a 13-13 April that felt like an audition rather than a statement. Neftalí Feliz was burned twice in high-leverage spots, surrendering blasts to Nolan Arenado and Addison Russell that set an anxious tone. But the month ended on a bright note, with Domingo Santana delivering a three-run shot that previewed what this lineup could do. May and June carried the season's heartbeat: big swings, big moments, and a roster that was discovering itself. Manny Piña's three-run blast in mid-May, Thames going deep in back-to-back games against the Pirates, and Keon Broxton answering a Padres go-ahead homer with one of his own all signaled that this Brewers team would not go quietly.\n\nJuly brought turbulence. The Brewers went 12-13, and their bullpen — so often the backbone of the Craig Counsell era — showed cracks. Corey Knebel was torched by a Clint Frazier three-run shot, and Jared Hughes gave up a pair of crushing late-game hits. But August roared back at 15-12, powered by timely heroics from Manny Piña and Jesús Aguilar, who emerged as a legitimate offensive force. The team refused to fold.\n\nSeptember was where the dream died slowly but beautifully. The Brewers went 15-12 in the final month, staying alive until the final days of the season, but the wild card spots were just out of reach. Josh Hader surrendered a gut-punch homer to Billy Hamilton, and Corey Knebel was victimized again in late September. Yet the month also gave fans Travis Shaw's 31st home run — a two-run moonshot on September 23rd that was both a celebration of an improbable season and a bittersweet farewell to playoff hopes. Milwaukee missed the postseason by the thinnest of margins, but the 2017 Brewers announced themselves as legitimate contenders for years to come.",
